SUMMARY:LMG seminar: Izzuddin Aris - Separating Algorithms From Questions 
 and Causal Inference With Unmeasured Exposures
LOCATION:Microsoft Teams / AUGC - G.07
DESCRIPTION:Longitudinal Modelling Group seminar\n\nSpeaker: Izzuddin Aris
  (Harvard Pilgrim Health Care Institute and Harvard Medical School)\n\nTi
 tle: Separating Algorithms From Questions and Causal Inference With Unmea
 sured Exposures: An Application to Birth Cohort Studies\n\nSummary: Obser
 vational studies have shown strong associations between BMI metrics in ch
 ildhood (e.g.\, early age at BMI rebound\, overweight/obesity) and subseq
 uent cardiometabolic outcomes including type 2 diabetes and metabolic syn
 drome in adolescence and adulthood. Despite adjustment for covariates\, t
 hese studies have not explicitly considered causal inference\, including 
 causal effect definitions and assumptions sufficient for unbiasedly estim
 ating that effect with available data. In this presentation\, we consider
  the problem of using existing observational data from a pre-birth cohort
  in USA (Project Viva) to estimate effects of stochastic interventions on
  a chosen subset of modifiable (but unmeasured) exposures\, expected to b
 e associated with BMI metrics (a proxy exposure)\, on adolescent cardiome
 tabolic and respiratory outcomes in early adolescence.\n\nJoin on Microso
 ft Teams: https://teams.microsoft.com/meet/34023039642214?p=qAf7nRZ0pRow8
